1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:07,486
Hello and welcome to the Owl's Lessons! Last time, we went through all the rules of backgammon.

2
00:00:07,186 --> 00:00:12,337
Today, we move on to practice: I'm going to teach you the best opening moves.

3
00:00:13,031 --> 00:00:15,916
The opening is the very first move of the game.

4
00:00:15,616 --> 00:00:19,052
And here's the good news: there are only fifteen to know.

5
00:00:18,752 --> 00:00:22,847
By the end of this lesson, you'll know how to play every one of them.

6
00:00:26,415 --> 00:00:32,603
Remember: to start, each player rolls a single die, and the higher roll plays with both dice.

7
00:00:33,448 --> 00:00:38,578
If the two dice are the same, you roll again. So the first move can never be a double.

8
00:00:40,002 --> 00:00:46,099
With two different dice, there are exactly fifteen combinations, from six-five down to two-one.

9
00:00:46,609 --> 00:00:52,636
Thanks to analysis programs, which have played millions of games, we now know the best move for each one.

10
00:00:52,336 --> 00:00:56,017
Learning them gives you a head start from the very first turn.

11
00:00:58,689 --> 00:01:01,048
Before we begin, a quick reminder.

12
00:01:00,748 --> 00:01:05,651
A move is always described from your point of view, using the point numbers.

13
00:01:07,124 --> 00:01:12,793
When I say "from eight to five", a checker leaves your eight-point and lands on your five-point.

14
00:01:13,667 --> 00:01:18,826
And when I say "two checkers from thirteen to eleven", the same move is played twice.

15
00:01:19,686 --> 00:01:25,599
The fifteen openings fall into three main families: making a point, running, and building.

16
00:01:25,299 --> 00:01:27,470
Let's start with the best one.

17
00:01:29,631 --> 00:01:32,751
Three-one is the finest opening in the game.

18
00:01:32,451 --> 00:01:36,724
Play one checker from eight to five, and one from six to five.

19
00:01:37,353 --> 00:01:40,375
Your two checkers end up together on the five-point.

20
00:01:40,075 --> 00:01:45,454
That point is now blocked for your opponent, and it's the most valuable point on the whole board.

21
00:01:46,125 --> 00:01:51,953
With four-two, same idea: from eight to four and from six to four. You make the four-point.

22
00:01:52,551 --> 00:01:57,509
With five-three, from eight to three and from six to three: you make the three-point.

23
00:01:57,209 --> 00:02:00,962
It's a little less useful, but it's still one more solid point.

24
00:02:01,677 --> 00:02:08,516
The owl's tip: whenever a roll lets you make a point in your home board right from the start, never pass it up!

25
00:02:12,488 --> 00:02:19,060
Six-one also makes a point, but a special one: the seven-point, which we call the bar point.

26
00:02:19,460 --> 00:02:23,862
Play one checker from thirteen to seven, and one from eight to seven.

27
00:02:24,012 --> 00:02:26,588
The seven-point sits right next to the bar.

28
00:02:26,288 --> 00:02:32,940
Together with your six- and eight-points, it already forms a three-point wall in front of your opponent's back checkers.

29
00:02:33,987 --> 00:02:39,781
It's the beginning of a prime, that famous six-point wall your opponent can't get past.

30
00:02:39,481 --> 00:02:42,244
And they'll soon realise they're stuck!

31
00:02:45,241 --> 00:02:46,928
Second family: running.

32
00:02:46,628 --> 00:02:52,356
Your two checkers on the twenty-four-point are far from home, and they risk being trapped.

33
00:02:53,925 --> 00:03:00,358
With six-five, it's easy: a single checker leaves the twenty-four and runs all the way to the thirteen.

34
00:03:00,058 --> 00:03:05,419
This move is called the lover's leap. Your checker is already safe, with its friends.

35
00:03:05,636 --> 00:03:13,024
With six-four, six-three and six-two, you usually play the six from twenty-four to eighteen, to bring a back checker out.

36
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:22,183
And you play the small die from the thirteen-point: thirteen to nine with the four, thirteen to ten with the three, thirteen to eleven with the two.

37
00:03:22,792 --> 00:03:30,390
With six-four and six-three, you can also run a single checker, from twenty-four to fourteen, or from twenty-four to fifteen.

38
00:03:30,090 --> 00:03:32,550
Both ways of playing are about equal.

39
00:03:34,450 --> 00:03:40,090
Third family: building. The other rolls don't let you make a point or run very far.

40
00:03:39,790 --> 00:03:42,020
So you place builders instead.

41
00:03:43,662 --> 00:03:48,527
A builder is a checker placed within reach of the points you want to make on your next turn.

42
00:03:49,343 --> 00:03:53,451
With five-two, play thirteen to eight and thirteen to eleven.

43
00:03:53,151 --> 00:03:57,509
With five-one, thirteen to eight and twenty-four to twenty-three.

44
00:03:57,923 --> 00:04:04,139
With four-three, two checkers come down from the thirteen, one to the nine and the other to the ten.

45
00:04:03,839 --> 00:04:07,630
With three-two, one to the ten and the other to the eleven.

46
00:04:07,780 --> 00:04:12,220
With four-one, thirteen to nine and twenty-four to twenty-three.

47
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:18,884
With five-four, thirteen to eight and thirteen to nine, or twenty-four to twenty and thirteen to eight.

48
00:04:19,034 --> 00:04:26,303
And with two-one, the smallest roll: thirteen to eleven, then twenty-four to twenty-three, or six to five.

49
00:04:26,974 --> 00:04:30,871
Yes, these moves sometimes leave a checker on its own, a blot.

50
00:04:30,571 --> 00:04:37,428
It's a small risk, but it's worth it: on your next turn, you'll have a much better chance of making a good point.

51
00:04:39,829 --> 00:04:42,456
A double never comes on the first move.

52
00:04:42,156 --> 00:04:48,243
But it can come on the second, in reply to your opponent's opening. Here are the classic replies.

53
00:04:49,401 --> 00:04:55,583
With double six, two checkers from twenty-four to eighteen, and two from thirteen to seven.

54
00:04:55,283 --> 00:04:58,169
You grab two very good points in one go.

55
00:04:58,319 --> 00:05:02,127
With double five, two checkers from thirteen to three.

56
00:05:01,827 --> 00:05:07,909
With double four, two checkers from twenty-four to twenty, and two from thirteen to nine.

57
00:05:08,059 --> 00:05:13,428
With double three, two checkers from eight to five and two from six to three.

58
00:05:13,128 --> 00:05:18,629
With double two, two checkers from thirteen to eleven and two from six to four.

59
00:05:18,779 --> 00:05:27,038
And with double one, two checkers from eight to seven and two from six to five: you make both the seven-point and the five-point!

60
00:05:28,938 --> 00:05:33,907
Careful: on the second move, everything also depends on what your opponent played.

61
00:05:34,541 --> 00:05:39,711
If they left a blot within reach of your dice, hitting it is often better than the classic move.

62
00:05:39,411 --> 00:05:43,870
Their checker goes back to the bar, and they lose all the ground they had covered.

63
00:05:44,470 --> 00:05:48,192
And don't try to learn everything by heart in one go.

64
00:05:47,892 --> 00:05:53,421
First remember the three ideas: make a point, run with a six, and place builders.

65
00:05:54,337 --> 00:05:56,476
The rest will come with practice.

66
00:05:56,176 --> 00:06:02,329
On TickGammon, the analysis of your games shows you when you stray from the best move, openings included.

67
00:06:05,147 --> 00:06:09,042
And there you go, you now know all fifteen backgammon openings!

68
00:06:08,742 --> 00:06:13,037
You'll find them all in the table on the Openings page, on TickGammon.

69
00:06:13,399 --> 00:06:20,410
To practise, play for free against other players or against the computer, in your browser, on Android or on Windows.

70
00:06:20,110 --> 00:06:25,733
Next time, I'll teach you how to use the doubling cube well. Subscribe so you don't miss it!

71
00:06:25,433 --> 00:06:27,469
See you soon around the board!

